"""waydroid-android-key -- deliver a key to Android from the host.

Installed at @BINDIR@/waydroid-android-key. Run as root, on the host.

    waydroid-android-key sleep    # Android's display off, keyguard arms
    waydroid-android-key wakeup   # display on, keyguard shown
    waydroid-android-key 116      # any raw evdev code

WHY THIS EXISTS AT ALL

The container gets no /dev/input and no /dev/uinput -- check config_nodes, there
is nothing of the kind in it. All Android input arrives over the Wayland socket:
the guest hwcomposer mkfifo()s three pipes and a patched InputFlinger reads them
as ordinary input devices. From `dumpsys input`, verified on this host:

    2: wayland_keyboard
      Classes: KEYBOARD | ALPHAKEY | DPAD
      Path: /dev/input/wl_keyboard_events
      KeyLayoutFile: /system/usr/keylayout/Generic.kl

That pipe is the injection point. Generic.kl carries `key 116 POWER`,
`key 142 SLEEP` and `key 143 WAKEUP`, so a write here is indistinguishable from
a real button as far as Android is concerned.

The host cannot simply open the path: the container's /dev is a tmpfs in its own
mount namespace. It is reachable as /proc/<any container pid>/root/dev/..., and
that traversal needs root -- even though the FIFO itself is prwxrwxrwx and owned
by uid 1000, which the host shares with the container.

WHY SLEEP/WAKEUP AND NOT POWER

Upstream's own doze work (android_hardware_waydroid, dev/lineage-23.2) injects
this same pair through this same pipe, and states the reason: KEY_POWER is a
toggle, so a lost or duplicated injection inverts the state and leaves the screen
off with no way back. KEY_SLEEP and KEY_WAKEUP are absolute, so they are safe to
send twice, and safe to send when Android is already in the target state. Every
retry in this tool and in the sleep hook depends on that property.

Note the guest drops KEY_POWER arriving over *Wayland* -- keyboard_handle_key()
returns early on it, on every upstream branch. That filter is on the compositor
path and does not apply to writes made here, so `waydroid-android-key power`
does work. It is offered for completeness and should not be used in automation.

THE FORMAT

send_key_event() in wayland-hwc.cpp writes ONE bare struct input_event per
transition -- no SYN_REPORT, unlike a real evdev device -- with a CLOCK_MONOTONIC
timestamp. This matches that exactly. Writes are 24 bytes, well under PIPE_BUF,
so they are atomic and cannot interleave with the hwcomposer's own.

See docs/27.
"""

import os
import struct
import sys
import time

# struct input_event on 64-bit: struct timeval {long, long}, __u16, __u16, __s32
EV_FMT = "llHHi"
EV_KEY = 0x01
assert struct.calcsize(EV_FMT) == 24

PIPE = "dev/input/wl_keyboard_events"

# Named for the two that are safe to automate; anything else takes a raw code.
KEYS = {"sleep": 142, "wakeup": 143, "power": 116}

# Any process inside the container will do -- all that is wanted is its mount
# namespace. surfaceflinger first: it is up before Android can show anything at
# all, and it is gone the moment the container is, so it never points at a stale
# namespace. system_server covers the sliver of early boot before it starts.
ANCHORS = ("surfaceflinger", "system_server", "zygote64", "init")


def find_pipe():
    """Return the host-visible path of the guest's keyboard FIFO, or None."""
    for name in ANCHORS:
        for pid in pids_named(name):
            path = "/proc/{}/root/{}".format(pid, PIPE)
            try:
                if os.path.exists(path):
                    return path
            except OSError:
                continue  # namespace vanished mid-scan; try the next one
    return None


def pids_named(comm):
    """Every pid whose comm is exactly `comm`. /proc walk, so no pgrep needed."""
    found = []
    for entry in os.listdir("/proc"):
        if not entry.isdigit():
            continue
        try:
            with open("/proc/{}/comm".format(entry)) as fh:
                if fh.read().strip() == comm:
                    found.append(entry)
        except OSError:
            continue
    return found


def send(path, code):
    """Write the down/up pair. Raises OSError if the pipe will not take them."""
    fd = os.open(path, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_NONBLOCK)
    try:
        now = time.clock_gettime(time.CLOCK_MONOTONIC)
        sec, usec = int(now), int((now % 1) * 1e6)
        for value in (1, 0):
            os.write(fd, struct.pack(EV_FMT, sec, usec, EV_KEY, code, value))
    finally:
        os.close(fd)


def main():
    if len(sys.argv) != 2:
        sys.exit("usage: waydroid-android-key sleep|wakeup|power|<evdev code>")

    arg = sys.argv[1]
    if arg in KEYS:
        code = KEYS[arg]
    elif arg.isdigit():
        code = int(arg)
    else:
        sys.exit("unknown key: {} (try sleep, wakeup, power or a number)".format(arg))

    path = find_pipe()
    if path is None:
        # Not an error: no container, or one too early in boot to have the pipe.
        # Callers in the suspend path must be able to treat this as "nothing to
        # do" without failing, so say so on stdout and exit 0.
        print("waydroid-android-key: no running container, nothing to do")
        return 0

    try:
        send(path, code)
    except PermissionError:
        sys.exit("waydroid-android-key: need root to reach {}".format(path))
    except OSError as exc:
        # ENXIO means the FIFO has no reader: InputFlinger has not opened it yet,
        # or Android is on its way down. Neither is worth failing a suspend over.
        print("waydroid-android-key: cannot write {}: {}".format(path, exc))
        return 0

    print("waydroid-android-key: sent {} ({}) via {}".format(arg, code, path))
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
